Problem Statement
Given an integer array nums and an integer k, return true if there are two distinct indices i and j in the array such that nums[i] == nums[j] and abs(i - j) <= k.
Example 1:
Input: nums = [1,2,3,1], k = 3
Output: true
Example 2:
Input: nums = [1,0,1,1], k = 1
Output: true
Example 3:
Input: nums = [1,2,3,1,2,3], k = 2
Output: false


1.Intuition
Unlike checking for any duplicate, here we need to ensure two duplicate values occur within a window of distance k (i.e., |i - j| <= k). By storing each element alongside its most recent index in a hash map, we can check in O(1) time whether a duplicate was seen recently enough to satisfy the distance condition.

2. Approach
   a)Initialize a hash map mp to store each unique number as a key and its latest array index as the value.
   b)Iterate through the array nums using index i:
       Check if nums[i] is already present in mp.
       If it exists and the difference between the current index i and its last seen index mp[nums[i]] is <= k, return true.
       Update the map with the current index: mp[nums[i]] = i (this ensures we always store the most recent position to maximize the chance of finding a pair within distance k).
3. If the loop completes without finding a valid pair, return false.
Complexity
  Time Complexity: O(n) where n is the length of nums, as each hash map lookup and insertion takes O(1) on average.
  Space Complexity: O(n) in the worst case to store up to n unique elements and their indices in the hash map. (Alternatively, using a sliding window of size k with unordered_set can reduce space complexity to O(min(n, k))).

3.Code
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    bool containsNearbyDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        unordered_map<int, int> mp;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (mp.count(nums[i])) {
                if (i - mp[nums[i]] <= k) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
            mp[nums[i]] = i;
        }

        return false;
    }
};
